extracting columns/var names:
1. Variable.columns -- prints all column names 
→ var.columns[2] – prints name of 2nd column
**belongs to pandas index object 
2. Dir(variable) → names the attributes/methods available on the object 


Chaining: A way to extract the names in a single line
**read from left to right. 
Ex: Car_cols = carfeatures.columns.values 
Extracts column names, and then turns those names into a numpy array
Could also do carfeatures.columns.values.tolist 
Changes the type to a list



Sorting by column/variable: can sort dataframes according to 1 of the variables:
Dataframe.sort_values 
→ ex: dataframe.sort_values(by = “mpg”)
**auto does ascending. If you want high to low do: datafrane.sortvalues( by = “mpg”, ascending = Fase)

Subsetting by row/column position: use .iloc 
**this is an attribute not a method (use square brackets) 
Carfeatures.iloc[0,0] → pulls the value from 1st row, 1st column
Order is ROW, COLUMNS. 
Use : if you want all features from one
When you do [1:6] it prints 1,2,3,4,5. Leaves the last number OUT
Last 3 rows: carfeatures.iloc[-3,:]
Second to last 2 rows: carfeatures.iloc[-3:-1, :]

Adding variables: Manipulating Data to add new vars/columns
First make the new var/column based on old ones
Then do dataset[“name”] = name to add this var to the df 
.apply applies your function to every var

Random assignment: to divide dataset into 4 random groups use numpy.random to assign each person 1,2,3,4:
(#rows, #columns) = Dataframe.shape
Group_no = np.random.choice([1,2,3,4], size = num_rows])
1,2,3,4 are possible group numbers
Size = num_rows tells numpy how many numbers to draw
dataframe[“groupID”] = group_no
